## Proof

Let the space curve be given by coordinates $x^i(t)$, $i = 1, \dots, n$, with parameter $t \in [t_1, t_2]$. The metric tensor $g_{ij}$ defines the squared line element

$$ds^2 = g_{ij}\, dx^i\, dx^j,$$

where Einstein summation is used over repeated indices.

Along the curve, $dx^i = \dot{x}^i\, dt$, where $\dot{x}^i = \frac{dx^i}{dt}$. Substituting,

$$ds^2 = g_{ij}\, \dot{x}^i \dot{x}^j\, dt^2,$$

so the infinitesimal arc length is

$$ds = \sqrt{g_{ij}\, \dot{x}^i \dot{x}^j}\; dt.$$

Integrating over the parameter range $[t_1, t_2]$, the total length of the curve is

$$\boxed{L = \int_{t_1}^{t_2} \sqrt{g_{ij}\, \frac{dx^i}{dt}\, \frac{dx^j}{dt}}\; dt.}$$

Here $g_{ij}$ is evaluated along the curve, i.e. $g_{ij}(x(t))$. In the special case of Euclidean space with Cartesian coordinates, $g_{ij} = \delta_{ij}$, and the formula reduces to the familiar

$$L = \int_{t_1}^{t_2} \sqrt{\sum_i \left(\frac{dx^i}{dt}\right)^2}\, dt.$$

